\tr{\td{Parallel 4 threads + \code{\verb~|-Xmn4g~}}\td{67.9}\td{35.2}\td{5 456}\td{2}}
\tr{\td{G1 (default collector)}\td{204.2}\td{53.7}\td{5 328}\td{10}}
\tr{\td{G1, \code{\verb~|ParallelGCThreads=1 ConcGCThreads=1~}}\td{312.1}\td{65.1}\td{2 848}\td{18}}
\tr{\td{G1, 4 parallel / 1 concurrent}\td{281.6}\td{66.8}\td{2 851}\td{18}}
\tr{\td{ZGC (generational)}\td{221.2}\td{105.0}\td{6 565}\td{42 cycles}}
\tr{\td{all defaults (G1, default sizing)}\td{262.8}\td{63.5}\td{3 221}\td{55}}
}

\p{Native images on the same workload: section 7.4.}

\ul{
\li{Serial costs 18.7 G over the mutator here, almost all of it for promoting the 40 M long-lived nodes once (about 470 instructions per promoted object including card scanning of the 512 MB table).}
\li{G1 and ZGC pay for barriers and remembered-set / concurrent work on every reference store into the large table; thread-count flags do not fix that.}
\li{Parallel GC spends more instructions than Serial at every thread count; more threads only shorten the pause.}
\li{CPU-time view on the bare host (20 cores, \code{\verb~|results/gc-matrix-aarch64.txt~}): Serial 33.5 s, Parallel 44.4 s, G1 43.3 s, ZGC 94.5 s, Epsilon 27.2 s: the ranking by CPU time understates G1's cost by 3 x.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{8.2 Design consequences}

\ol{
\li{Objects that live for the whole run should not be small Java objects created in the young generation: each is copied once (about 470 instructions) and its table slot dirties a card. Packed \code{\verb~|int[]~} storage is copied by \code{\verb~|memmove~} at most once per array instead of object by object (cost of large-array promotion not measured separately).}
\li{Size the young generation so that the number of collections is small (2-10 for the whole run).}
\li{Fixed heap (\code{\verb~|-Xms~} = most of \code{\verb~|-Xmx~}): growth steps cost collections.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{8.3 Other flags}

\table{
\tr{\th{flag}\th{finding}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|-XX:+AlwaysPreTouch~}}\td{+4.3 s system time, RSS 8.5 GB instead of 5.7 GB; page faults are kernel instructions and are not counted, so pre-touching buys nothing and costs RSS. Do not use.}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|-XX:+UseTransparentHugePages~}}\td{CPU time -4 \% (30.6 vs 31.8 s); instruction effect not measured; host THP mode is \code{\verb~|madvise~}, the runner's is unknown. Harmless.}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|-XX:-UseAdaptiveSizePolicy~}}\td{only relevant to Parallel and not isolated: 4 threads with \code{\verb~|-Xmn4g~} and adaptive sizing off used 31.4 s CPU against 36.1 s with defaults}}
\tr{\td{compressed oops / class pointers}\td{on by default below 32 GB; \code{\verb~|-XX:-UseCompressedOops~} (or a heap above 32 GB) makes a 2-ref node 32 B instead of 24 B. Stay below 32 GB.}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|-XX:+UseStringDeduplication~}}\td{G1/ZGC/Shenandoah feature; irrelevant with Serial. Intern names once in the parser instead.}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|-XX:+UseCompactObjectHeaders~} (JEP 519, product in 25)}\td{works with Serial, C2, the Graal JIT via libgraal, Truffle runtime compilation in JVM mode, and on the Oracle JDK (all run). Costs +1.6 \% (GcBench) to +3 \% (plain interpreter) instructions.}}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{8.4 Object sizes (bytes; exact allocation counter on HotSpot, Epsilon heap delta in the image)}

\table{
\tr{\th{class}\th{HotSpot default}\th{compact headers}\th{no compressed oops}\th{native-image}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|Object~}}\td{16}\td{8}\td{16}\td{8}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|{int}~}}\td{16}\td{16}\td{16}\td{16}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|{long}~} / boxed \code{\verb~|Long~}}\td{24}\td{16}\td{24}\td{16}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|{ref}~}}\td{16}\td{16}\td{24}\td{16}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|{ref, ref}~}}\td{24}\td{16}\td{32}\td{16}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|{byte, ref, ref}~}}\td{24}\td{24}\td{32}\td{24}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|{ref, ref, int}~}}\td{24}\td{24}\td{32}\td{24}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|{ref, ref, int, int}~}}\td{32}\td{24}\td{40}\td{24}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|{ref, ref, ref}~}}\td{24}\td{24}\td{40}\td{24}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|{ref, ref, ref, int}~}}\td{32}\td{24}\td{40}\td{24}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|{ref x4}~}}\td{32}\td{24}\td{48}\td{24}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|{long, ref}~}}\td{24}\td{24}\td{32}\td{24}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|int[0]~}}\td{16}\td{16}\td{16}\td{8}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|int[3]~} / \code{\verb~|int[4]~}}\td{32 / 32}\td{24 / 32}\td{32 / 32}\td{24 / 24}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|Object[1]~} / \code{\verb~|[2]~} / \code{\verb~|[4]~}}\td{24 / 24 / 32}\td{16 / 24 / 32}\td{24 / 32 / 48}\td{16 / 16 / 24}}
\tr{\td{lambda capturing 1 / 2 refs}\td{16 / 24}\td{16 / 16}\td{24 / 32}\td{16 / 16}}
}

\p{Headers: HotSpot 12 B (arrays 16 B), compact 8 B (arrays 12 B), native-image 8 B (arrays 8 B; the identity hash is appended only when used, \code{\verb~|-H:+OptionalIdentityHashCodes~}). Layout rule: with an 8 B header a node with two references and no extra field is 16 B; a \code{\verb~|byte~} tag field costs a whole 8 B. Use the class as the tag, or pack the tag into an existing \code{\verb~|int~}.}
}

\subtree{
\title{8.5 Packed storage access (ns per 12-byte record; sequential scan / random chase)}

\table{
\tr{\th{store}\th{HotSpot C2}\th{HotSpot Graal JIT}\th{native CE}\th{native CE, holder class initialised at build time}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|int[]~}}\td{0.57 / 3.6}\td{0.70 / 3.4}\td{0.91 / 3.8}\td{0.94 / 3.8}}
\tr{\td{heap \code{\verb~|IntBuffer~}}\td{1.83 / 4.6}\td{0.99 / 3.8}\td{4.14 / 4.6}\td{4.09 / 4.6}}
\tr{\td{direct \code{\verb~|IntBuffer~}}\td{1.89 / 4.2}\td{0.72 / 3.7}\td{3.89 / 4.6}\td{3.90 / 4.6}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|MemorySegment.ofArray~}}\td{3.40 / 4.4}\td{0.86 / 4.2}\td{\strong{141 / 148}}\td{17.2 / 17.2}}
\tr{\td{native \code{\verb~|MemorySegment~}}\td{5.01 / 5.2}\td{0.82 / 3.6}\td{\strong{143 / 151}}\td{17.3 / 17.3}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|byte[]~} + \code{\verb~|VarHandle~}}\td{0.57 / 3.9}\td{0.76 / 4.0}\td{\strong{128 / 130}}\td{0.99 / 4.1}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|Unsafe~} on a native address}\td{0.57 / 4.2}\td{0.51 / 3.3}\td{2.0 / 3.9}\td{1.9 / 3.6}}
}

\p{(20 M records, 228 MB; times, not instructions.) \code{\verb~|int[]~} is the fastest or tied everywhere, needs no flags, and its bounds checks are hoisted by both JITs. Off-heap storage buys nothing for access speed; its only advantage is that the GC never sees it, which large \code{\verb~|int[]~} arrays nearly achieve.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{9. Deep recursion (question 6)}

\subtree{
\title{9.1 Limits and frame sizes (\code{\verb~|DeepRec~}, a 3-local recursive step that allocates)}

\table{
\tr{\th{runtime}\th{bytes per frame}\th{default stack}\th{8 GB thread stack}}
\tr{\td{HotSpot C2}\td{32}\td{\code{\verb~|ThreadStackSize=2040~} KB on this aarch64 build; overflow at depth 15-17 k while still interpreted}\td{depth 268 M reached}}
\tr{\td{HotSpot Graal JIT}\td{48}\td{same}\td{1 GB tested: depth 22 M}}
\tr{\td{HotSpot C1}\td{112}\td{}\td{}}
\tr{\td{HotSpot interpreter}\td{160}\td{}\td{}}
\tr{\td{native-image CE}\td{64}\td{main thread uses \code{\verb~|ulimit -s~} (8 MB here): depth 130 k}\td{depth 134 M reached}}
}

\ul{
\li{\code{\verb~|new Thread(null, runnable, name, stackSizeBytes)~} honours multi-gigabyte sizes on both runtimes; only touched pages are committed (10 M frames x 32 B = 320 MB of RSS). \code{\verb~|-Xss1g~} also enlarges the Java main thread on HotSpot (depth 33 M). \code{\verb~|refs/tenet-jvm/src/main/java/tenet/Main.java:47~} uses \code{\verb~|4L << 30~}.}
\li{\code{\verb~|StackOverflowError~} is catchable and the thread stays usable (four consecutive overflows tested on both). Cost: about 100-130 ns per unwound frame on HotSpot (33 M frames: 3.4 s per overflow), about the same on SVM; the first descent also pays page faults.}
\li{\strong{Anomaly with the Graal JIT (CE 25.5.5.0.3 and Oracle 25.0.4), reproducible 3 of 3 runs}: after one \code{\verb~|StackOverflowError~} on the main thread, a later thread with a 64 MB stack threw \code{\verb~|StackOverflowError~} at depth of about 1 000 in freshly compiled code. It does not happen with \code{\verb~|-XX:-UseJVMCICompiler~}, with \code{\verb~|-XX:-BackgroundCompilation~}, or when the first overflow is skipped (\code{\verb~|-Ddeeprec.skipMain=true~}). Not root-caused. Another reason to use C2, and to use an explicit depth counter rather than catching \code{\verb~|StackOverflowError~} as control flow.}
\li{Truffle frames: a guest call in the AST interpreter is several host frames (\code{\verb~|callBoundary~}, \code{\verb~|executeRootNode~}, \code{\verb~|execute~} of each node on the path), so interpreted guest recursion is far shallower per megabyte than host recursion; compiled guest frames were 64-160 B in the spike (\code{\verb~|Total frame size~} in the compilation statistics).}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{9.2 Deep stacks and GC (NbeBench \code{\verb~|nat10m-norm~} x5: quote recursion 10 M frames deep)}

\table{
\tr{\th{configuration}\th{G}\th{collections}}
\tr{\td{JVM C2, Epsilon}\td{14.3}\td{0}}
\tr{\td{JVM C2, Serial \code{\verb~|-Xmn4g~}}\td{16.7}\td{1}}
\tr{\td{JVM C2, Serial \code{\verb~|-Xmn1g~}}\td{68.7}\td{5}}
\tr{\td{JVM C2, Serial \code{\verb~|-Xmn256m~}}\td{87.1}\td{21}}
\tr{\td{JVM C2, Parallel \code{\verb~|-Xmn1g~}}\td{79.3}\td{5}}
\tr{\td{JVM C2, G1}\td{102.3}\td{8}}
\tr{\td{JVM C2, ZGC}\td{94.7}\td{16}}
\tr{\td{JVM \code{\verb~|-Xint~}, Serial \code{\verb~|-Xmn256m~}, one iteration}\td{63.5}\td{4}}
\tr{\td{native CE, Epsilon}\td{18.1}\td{0}}
\tr{\td{native CE, \code{\verb~|-Xmn4g~}}\td{18.1}\td{1}}
\tr{\td{native CE, \code{\verb~|-Xmn1g~}}\td{51.5}\td{7}}
\tr{\td{native CE, \code{\verb~|-Xmn256m~}}\td{234.1}\td{43}}
}

\p{A collection that happens while the stack is 10 M frames deep costs about 10 G on HotSpot (about 1 000 instructions per frame) and about 5 G on SVM. With default heap settings the first iteration of the HOAS variant took 7.3 s instead of 0.09 s because the deep frames were still interpreted.}

\p{Rules: (1) recursion on the host stack is fine and cheapest per step while collections are rare; (2) for traversals whose depth follows the data (long application spines, long \code{\verb~|let~} chains, deep binder telescopes) use loops or explicit worklists held in arrays; (3) never combine a small young generation with deep recursion.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{10. HOAS / NbE on the JVM: the two normalization-bench repositories, re-measured}

\subtree{
\title{10.1 What the repositories say (platform-relevant parts)}

\p{\code{\verb~|refs/normalization-bench-kovacs/README.md~} (Scala HOAS on OpenJDK 8 and GraalVM 21, 2020):}

\ul{
\li{JVM HOAS was the best of the JIT platforms and competitive with GHC on the tree benchmarks, but "performance ... degrades sharply from 5M to 10M with natural numbers; perhaps there is some issue with deep stacks" (lines 115-119); GraalVM made that case worse.}
\li{It needed \code{\verb~|-Xss1000M -Xmx4G~} (line 28); "Scala and nodejs performance becomes dismal without free RAM" (lines 157-160); occasional multi-second pauses and memory spikes (lines 164-169).}
\li{All JIT entries are call-by-value; call-by-need was not measured.}
}

\p{\code{\verb~|refs/normalization-bench-guest0x0/README.md~} (OCaml, many normaliser variants):}

\ul{
\li{Plain untyped NbE with closures and a linked-list environment (\code{\verb~|NBE.closure.list~}) is among the fastest everywhere; list environments beat balanced trees and skew lists (line 370).}
\li{HOAS and first-order closures perform the same in OCaml (line 388); the lazy variant is slower (line 395).}
\li{An abstract machine with heap-allocated continuations has a stable constant overhead against recursion on the native stack (line 406).}
\li{A memo slot for the quoted term is nearly free when stored inside the value object (variant \code{\verb~|v3~}, one indirection, line 563) and expensive behind a wrapper.}
\li{Full hash-consing has a significant overhead except under extreme sharing (line 585); sharing only leaf nodes is a cheap, reliable win (line 592).}
\li{Compiling terms through the host compiler is impractical because of compile time (lines 686, 729).}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{10.2 Re-measurement on JDK 25 (\code{\verb~|plain/NbeBench.java~})}

\p{Kovacs' table, best of 10 iterations in ms, JVM defaults (G1, Graal JIT), this host:}

\table{
\tr{\th{workload}\th{hoas}\th{tag}\th{virt}\th{pat}\th{Scala 2020 (README)}}
\tr{\td{tree 2M normalisation}\td{33.5}\td{66.1}\td{58.0}\td{60.8}\td{85}}
\tr{\td{tree 2M conversion}\td{119.7}\td{100.1}\td{114.1}\td{101.8}\td{118}}
\tr{\td{nat 5M normalisation}\td{85.3}\td{104.5}\td{98.6}\td{104.6}\td{374}}
\tr{\td{nat 5M conversion}\td{76.8}\td{100.6}\td{87.4}\td{98.7}\td{138}}
}

\p{Mutator instructions (Epsilon GC, tree 4M normalisation x10):}

\table{
\tr{\th{evaluator}\th{JVM C2}\th{native CE}\th{native Oracle PGO (trained on \code{\verb~|tag~})}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|hoas~} (Java lambdas)}\td{12.35}\td{14.14}\td{22.97}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|tag~} (closure = env + term, \code{\verb~|switch~} on a byte tag)}\td{18.75}\td{25.40}\td{24.13}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|virt~} (virtual \code{\verb~|eval~} method per term class)}\td{20.73}\td{24.61}\td{27.07}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|pat~} (sealed hierarchy, pattern \code{\verb~|switch~})}\td{19.95}\td{26.12}\td{36.59}}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{10.3 Platform conclusions}

\ol{
\li{The deep-stack degradation Kovacs saw is the stack-walking cost of section 9.2, plus interpreted frames early in the run. It disappears with a large young generation (\code{\verb~|-Xmn4g~}: 16.7 G against 14.3 G without any GC) and gets worse with G1.}
\li{"Needs free RAM" is the same effect as section 8.1: collection count, not heap size, is what costs. Fixed heap plus large young generation is the cure.}
\li{On the JVM, host-closure HOAS is 34 \% cheaper than the environment machine, unlike in OCaml, because the lambda bodies are JIT-compiled Java. It applies only to code that exists as Java at build time (primitives, recursor schemes, built-in reductions). For terms that arrive at run time the equivalent is a closure tree (\code{\verb~|PlainInterp~}) or generated classes, whose compile cost is the Truffle economics of section 5.}
\li{Dispatch style matters little on HotSpot (within 10 \%); a \code{\verb~|switch~} on a small tag is the cheapest, and the Java 21+ pattern \code{\verb~|switch~} is fine. In a native image virtual dispatch was marginally best.}
\li{Value layout advice carries over directly: put cache slots in the value object itself, share leaf nodes through preallocated arrays, and avoid wrapper objects. Section 8.4 gives the sizes.}
\li{Guest0x0's verdict on compile-to-host matches our numbers: about 1 M instructions per AST node compiled.}
}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{11. Packaging for the arena (question 7)}

\subtree{
\title{11.1 Facts about the runner}

\ul{
\li{Runner label \code{\verb~|nscloud-ubuntu-22.04-amd64-8x16-with-cache~} (\code{\verb~|refs/lean-kernel-arena/.github/workflows/build-and-deploy.yml:89~}); steps run in \code{\verb~|nix develop -c bash~}. Generic glibc binaries such as a downloaded GraalVM should therefore run (Ubuntu host loader); inferred, not tested on the runner.}
\li{\code{\verb~|flake.nix~} lists \code{\verb~|jdk~}; in the pinned nixpkgs (\code{\verb~|flake.lock~} rev \code{\verb~|0ad6f47e...~}, \code{\verb~|pkgs/top-level/all-packages.nix:4051~}) \strong{\code{\verb~|jdk = jdk21~}}. OpenJDK 21 has no Graal compiler, no compact headers and no AOT cache. \code{\verb~|checkers/tenet-jvm.yaml~} uses it with plain \code{\verb~|javac~}.}
\li{Build steps may download: \code{\verb~|checkers/rpylean.yaml~} clones pypy, \code{\verb~|checkers/gruszka.yaml~} and \code{\verb~|pestka.yaml~} fetch a training file with \code{\verb~|curl~} and check it with \code{\verb~|sha256sum -c~}, \code{\verb~|checkers/kefir-wasm.yaml~} installs rustup.}
\li{Measurement: \code{\verb~|perf stat -e instructions~} around GNU \code{\verb~|time~} around \code{\verb~|sh -c "$RUN"~} (\code{\verb~|refs/lean-kernel-arena/lka.py:496-501~}), all child processes and threads included.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{11.2 Build and run commands}

\p{Tested end to end in the arena-shaped container on aarch64 (\src{spikes/platform/arena/build.sh}, \code{\verb~|run.sh~}; 13.5 s for the JVM build, 1 min 57 s with \code{\verb~|RLVM_NATIVE=1~}). Checker YAML:}

\pre{\verb~|
build: sh arena/build.sh .
run: sh arena/run.sh "$IN"
~}

\p{\code{\verb~|build.sh~}, in outline (the script in the spike is complete and runnable):}

\pre{\verb~|
case "$(uname -m)" in
  x86_64)  ARCH=x64;     GRAAL_SHA=c6438dee6c3ade2220d40b7ccdcad24b512d30e233e61bb4fc70519522de34ef ;;
  aarch64) ARCH=aarch64; GRAAL_SHA=41741c0650ae7564a78335f9e4fc353a902e6ff1d4140f2e4fabd1c55d5cce66 ;;
esac
F=graalvm-community-jdk-25i5-25.0.5.0.3_linux-${ARCH}_bin.tar.gz
curl -sSfL --retry 3 -o .build/dl/$F https://github.com/graalvm/graalvm-ce-builds/releases/download/graal-25.5.5.0.3/$F
echo "$GRAAL_SHA  .build/dl/$F" | sha256sum -c -
tar -xzf .build/dl/$F -C .build/graalvm --strip-components=1
# 9 jars from https://repo1.maven.org/maven2/org/graalvm/{polyglot,sdk,truffle}/..., each sha256-checked
javac --release 25 -cp "$CP" -processorpath .build/proc/truffle-dsl-processor-25.5.5.0.3.jar -s .build/gen -d .build/classes @.build/sources.txt
jar --create --file .build/rlvm.jar -C .build/classes .
~}

\p{Pinned jar checksums (25.5.5.0.3): polyglot \code{\verb~|51096bb4...~}, collections \code{\verb~|694c52b4...~}, nativeimage \code{\verb~|b8d337a9...~}, word \code{\verb~|11a21c7f...~}, jniutils \code{\verb~|1c46ac4d...~}, truffle-api \code{\verb~|ce6662ca...~}, truffle-runtime \code{\verb~|a4b8df50...~}, truffle-compiler \code{\verb~|f12a579e...~}, truffle-dsl-processor \code{\verb~|39daafb0...~} (full values in \code{\verb~|arena/build.sh~}). The x64 GraalVM checksum is the digest published by the GitHub release API; the aarch64 one was verified against the local archive.}

\p{Measured in the container with the recommended flags: \code{\verb~|ast tree 20~} x200 29.6 G (JVM) and 19.8 G (native); \code{\verb~|plain tree 20~} x200 321.9 G (JVM, C2 only) and 379.8 G (native CE).}
}

\subtree{
\title{11.3 Reproducibility and offline concerns}

\ul{
\li{Every download is pinned by sha256; the script re-verifies cached files and supports \code{\verb~|RLVM_OFFLINE=1~}. Vendoring the 9 jars (24 MB) in the repository removes the Maven Central dependency; the GraalVM archive (351 MB for x64) is too large to vendor.}
\li{GitHub release assets and Maven Central artifacts are immutable; Oracle's \code{\verb~|archive/~} URLs are versioned, \code{\verb~|latest/~} is not: never use \code{\verb~|latest~}.}
\li{The build output is about 1 GB (unpacked JDK 635 MB). Delete the archive after unpacking.}
\li{JIT-compiled runs are not bit-reproducible in instruction count (about 1 \%).}
\li{If RLVM does not use Truffle at all, the checker could run on the arena's own \code{\verb~|jdk~} 21 with zero downloads (as tenet-jvm does), giving up compact headers and JDK 25 improvements. Not measured.}
\li{Native image only: the link step needs \code{\verb~|gcc~} and zlib in the \code{\verb~|nix develop~} environment. zlib is not listed in \code{\verb~|flake.nix~}; whether it is reachable (for example propagated by \code{\verb~|libllvm~}) was not verified. The infra team's Ubuntu-based container has \code{\verb~|zlib1g-dev~}, so success there proves nothing about the runner.}
}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{12. Risks, unverified items, open questions}

\p{Unverified or out of reach on this host:}

\ul{
\li{x86-64 instruction counts and the behaviour of \code{\verb~|-march~} / default x86-64-v3 targets.}
\li{OpenJDK 21 from nixpkgs with or without Truffle.}
\li{Whether the arena's nix shell can link a native image (zlib, gcc wrapper).}
\li{\code{\verb~|truffle-enterprise~} on the class path; explicit \code{\verb~|compile()~} inside a native image; \code{\verb~|LoopNode.reportLoopCount~} as a hotness channel; Bytecode DSL continuations and serialization.}
\li{The cause of the Graal JIT \code{\verb~|StackOverflowError~} anomaly and of the 25.0.4 deopt loop.}
\li{JIT overhead of the real checker: estimated between the two measured extremes, not measured.}
}

\p{Open questions for the design team:}

\ol{
\li{Is Truffle needed at all? On these spikes a plain-Java closure-tree interpreter beats both Truffle interpreters before compilation, and compilation pays only above about 10^5 executions per function. What fraction of mathlib's reduction work sits in definitions that hot?}
\li{If Truffle stays: does the kernel drive compilation explicitly (internal API) or through raised global thresholds?}
\li{May an arena build download Oracle GraalVM under GFTC? If not, configuration C is unavailable.}
\li{How much of the long-lived term store can be \code{\verb~|int[]~}-packed, so that GC cost approaches the Epsilon line?}
\li{Is \code{\verb~|-XX:-TieredCompilation~} still a win on the real checker?}
}
}

